{"causes":["The fall of the Western Roman Empire created a context ripe for philosophical inquiry.","The rise of Christian thought necessitated a synthesis with classical philosophy.","Boethius' personal misfortunes and imprisonment prompted deep philosophical reflection."],"effects":["The text became a foundational work for medieval scholasticism and Christian philosophy.","It influenced key thinkers such as Thomas Aquinas and Geoffrey Chaucer.","The Consolation of Philosophy was instrumental in the revival of classical texts during the Renaissance."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"boethius","sourceName":"Boethius","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"the-consolation-of-philosophy","targetName":"The Consolation of Philosophy","context":"Boethius authored The Consolation of Philosophy during his imprisonment around 524 CE."},{"sourceSlug":"the-consolation-of-philosophy","sourceName":"The Consolation of Philosophy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"thomas-aquinas","targetName":"Thomas Aquinas","context":"The Consolation of Philosophy significantly influenced Thomas Aquinas in the 13th century."},{"sourceSlug":"the-consolation-of-philosophy","sourceName":"The Consolation of Philosophy","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"medieval-philosophy","targetName":"Medieval Philosophy","context":"The text transmitted ancient philosophical ideas into medieval thought, shaping the discourse of the era."},{"sourceSlug":"the-consolation-of-philosophy","sourceName":"The Consolation of Philosophy","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"dante-alighieri","targetName":"Dante Alighieri","context":"Dante Alighieri drew upon the themes of The Consolation of Philosophy in his works, notably in The Divine Comedy."},{"sourceSlug":"the-consolation-of-philosophy","sourceName":"The Consolation of Philosophy","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"scholasticism","targetName":"Scholasticism","context":"The Consolation of Philosophy was integral in defining the framework of scholasticism during the Middle Ages."}],"places":[{"name":"Rome, Italy","role":"Place of Composition"},{"name":"Ravenna, Italy","role":"Place of Imprisonment"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Major Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Consolation of Philosophy","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-consolation-of-philosophy"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q861927","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On_the_Consolation_of_Philosophy"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Consolation of Philosophy contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Consolation of Philosophy (Latin: De Consolatione Philosophiae) is a philosophical dialogue in prose and verse by the Roman statesman and philosopher Anicius Manlius Severinus Boethius (c. 480–524 CE), composed in prison while awaiting execution on charges of treason under the Ostrogothic King Theoderic. Written in the Menippean satire tradition (alternating prose and verse — 39 metrical poems interspersed with prose dialogue), it imagines the allegorical figure of Lady Philosophy visiting Boethius in his cell, consoling him for his fall from fortune and leading him through a philosophical dialogue on the nature of true happiness, the instability of fortune, divine providence, and the reconciliation of free will with God's foreknowledge. The work draws on Platonic, Neoplatonic, Stoic, and Aristotelian traditions.

The Consolation of Philosophy was the most widely read philosophical text in medieval Europe after the Bible — translated into Old English by King Alfred the Great (c. 888 CE), into Middle English by Chaucer (c. 1380), into French by Jean de Meun (c. 1300), and commented upon by virtually every major medieval philosopher (Remigius of Auxerre, William of Conches, Thomas Aquinas). Its central concept — the Wheel of Fortune (the goddess Fortuna constantly turning her wheel, raising some and lowering others) — became the defining medieval metaphor for the instability of worldly prosperity and one of the most enduring images in Western culture. Its argument for the reconciliation of divine foreknowledge with human free will was central to medieval theological debate.

The Consolation's remarkable literary achievement — the equanimity and philosophical depth of a man composing a philosophical masterpiece while awaiting execution — has given it a special place in the literature of adversity: Boethius's figure is invoked in discussions of how to face death and reversal, and the Consolation has remained in print virtually continuously for 1,500 years.","subjects":["Latin Philosophy","Late Antique Era","Boethius","Medieval Philosophy","Stoicism","Neoplatonism","Fortune","Prison Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Consolation of Philosophy played a pivotal role in the transition from classical to medieval thought, influencing generations of philosophers and shaping the intellectual framework of the Middle Ages.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
